---
import ToolPage from '../../layouts/ToolPage.astro';
import CanISeed from '../../components/CanISeed.astro';
import SeedWatch from '../../components/SeedWatch.astro';
const faq = [
  ['What is the best time to plant grass seed in the Northeast?', 'September. The soil is still warm, so seed sprouts fast, the nights are cooling off, and weeds are slowing down. Late August can work in a cool year, but around here August heat usually beats up new grass. Spring is possible but I don’t recommend it: new grass sprouts with the crabgrass and has to survive its first summer.'],
  ['What soil temperature does grass seed need?', 'Cool-season grass seed sprouts best with soil between about 50 and 70°F. Below 50°F it barely moves. Very hot soil and air make it hard to keep seedlings alive.'],
  ['Is it too late to plant grass seed in October?', 'Early October can still work with fast seed like perennial ryegrass or tall fescue if the soil is above about 50°F. After mid-October, skip seeding and plan for next September.'],
  ['Should I seed in November or over the winter?', 'No. Seed won’t sprout in cold soil, and seed left sitting all winter gets eaten by birds or washed away. Save your money and seed next September.'],
  ['Can I seed in spring and use crabgrass preventer?', 'Usually not in the same spot. Most crabgrass preventers stop grass seed too. Skip the preventer where you seed, or use mesotrione (sold as Tenacity), which is labeled for use at seeding. <a href="/guides/weed-killer-and-grass-seed/">More on weed killer and seed</a>.'],
];
const sources = [
  ['Penn State Extension: Lawn management through the seasons', 'https://extension.psu.edu/lawn-management-through-the-seasons'],
  ['Iowa State Extension: Late summer and fall lawn care', 'https://yardandgarden.extension.iastate.edu/how-to/late-summer-and-fall-lawn-care'],
];
---
<ToolPage seo="Can I Plant Grass Seed Now? Live Answer by ZIP" crumb="Can I Seed Now?" slug="can-i-seed-now" appName="Can I Seed Now?"
  h1="Can I Plant Grass Seed Now?"
  description="A live yes-or-no answer on planting grass seed this week, from your soil temperature and 7-day forecast. Plus germination times and a seed countdown."
  answer="<b>In the Northeast, the best time to plant grass seed is September, when soil is 50 to 70°F and the nights are cooling off.</b> Late August works in a cool year. Spring is possible, but <a href='/blog/spring-seeding/'>I don’t recommend it</a>. The live checker below looks at your soil temperature and this week’s forecast and gives you a straight yes or no."
  faq={faq} sources={sources}>
  <Fragment slot="tool"><CanISeed /><div class="tp-extra"><SeedWatch guide={false} /></div></Fragment>

  <h2>How It Decides</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Time of year.</strong> September beats everything. Late August only if the heat breaks early. Spring is possible but not recommended. Summer is a fight. Late fall and winter: skip it.</li>
    <li><strong>Soil temperature now and next week.</strong> Seed wants about 50 to 70°F.</li>
    <li><strong>Heat.</strong> Highs in the upper 80s cook baby grass.</li>
    <li><strong>Rain.</strong> Some rain is free watering. A downpour washes seed into the gutter.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How Long Until It Comes Up</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Grass</th><th>Sprouts In</th><th>First Mow</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Perennial ryegrass</td><td>5 to 10 days</td><td>About 4 weeks</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Tall fescue</td><td>7 to 14 days</td><td>4 to 5 weeks</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fine fescue (shade mixes)</td><td>7 to 14 days</td><td>4 to 6 weeks</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Kentucky bluegrass</td><td>14 to 30 days</td><td>6 weeks or more</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Already seeded? Use the “Is My Grass Seed Working?” countdown up top and I’ll tell you when to stop worrying.</p>
  <p>Not sure which seed to buy? <a href="/lawn-101/#grass">Pick your grass in four questions</a>. Full steps: <a href="/guides/when-to-overseed-lawn/">when and how to overseed</a> and <a href="/guides/fix-bare-spots-in-lawn/">fixing bare spots</a>.</p>
</ToolPage>
